{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794527770944,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","part":{"id":"prt_1d230b940001ozHqkFaHE6wUjI","sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","messageID":"msg_1d230ad79001Mo5VbMBxPKrdIb","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"b8bde3ff4b40657c5b69d84ee2061145b3b7a599","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":453,"output":64,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":50944,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794527774596,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","part":{"id":"prt_1d230c784001d5Z6La7gevoKLk","sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","messageID":"msg_1d230b944001MKvUUH3h0wjMES","type":"step-start","snapshot":"4d51389a63e05dda0a9d22ab446f194cf58dd3b8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527776711,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","part":{"partID":"prt_1d230cd72001CvdS9gWU6k0QUd","sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","messageID":"msg_1d230b944001MKvUUH3h0wjMES","type":"tool","id":"call_9406916b37e34196a86b215a","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527776114,"end":1794527776711}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527776714,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","part":{"partID":"prt_1d230cbe6002zzoDjFUsbaZ2NV","sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","messageID":"msg_1d230b944001MKvUUH3h0wjMES","type":"tool","id":"call_bed531ffdcfb42419b6b887a","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md, lines 1-162\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Root cause and debugging\"\n3: description: \"Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"fix the producer not the symptom\"\n6:   - \"name the mechanism\"\n7:   - \"symptom fix smells\"\n8:   - \"special case\"\n9:   - \"add a retry\"\n10:   - \"add a sleep\"\n11:   - \"broadened catch\"\n12:   - \"loosen the assertion\"\n13:   - \"never weaken a check\"\n14:   - \"make the red green\"\n15:   - \"it's a flake\"\n16:   - \"infra problem\"\n17:   - \"pre-existing failure\"\n18:   - \"not caused by my change\"\n19:   - \"second attempt at the same fix\"\n20:   - \"level up to the seam\"\n21:   - \"stuck escalation format\"\n22:   - \"flaky test\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.2\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Root cause and debugging\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when something is red.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Anything fails: a test, a build, a check, a runtime error, a wrong output.\n36: - You are on the second attempt at the same fix.\n37: - About to add a special case, a retry, a sleep, a broadened `catch`, or a loosened assertion.\n38: - You are tempted to call a failure a flake, an infra problem, or pre-existing.\n39: \n40: ## The rule\n41: \n42: **Fix the producer, not the symptom. Every fix names the mechanism that caused the\n43: failure.**\n44: \n45: If you cannot say *why* it broke in one sentence, you have not found the cause, you\n46: have found a place where the symptom stops appearing.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE LOOP\n51: \n52: 1. **Reproduce the exact symptom** safely. Not a similar one, the same failure, the\n53:    same message.\n54: 2. **Read the whole error.** The top line names where it surfaced; the cause is usually\n55:    the innermost frame in code you control, and the message is usually literal.\n56: 3. **Locate the producer:** the code that created the bad value or state, not the code\n57:    that choked on it.\n58: 4. **Trace to the consumers.** Anything else reading that producer has the same bug,\n59:    surfaced or not.\n60: 5. **Fix at the source.**\n61: 6. **Re-run the same check that failed**, then the whole gate.\n62: \n63: Step 1 is not optional. If you cannot reproduce it you cannot prove you fixed it, and\n64: you should say so instead of claiming a fix (see the negative control in\n65: `evidence-and-proof.md`).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. SYMPTOM-FIX SMELLS\n70: \n71: Each is evidence you are patching in the wrong place:\n72: \n73: | Smell | What it is really telling you |\n74: |-------|-------------------------------|\n75: | A special case for one caller | The seam between the module and that caller is wrong |\n76: | A retry around a deterministic operation | You do not know why it fails, and now it fails five times |\n77: | A `sleep` | A real ordering or lifecycle bug, now timing-dependent |\n78: | A broadened `catch` or bare `except` | The failure is now silent and the wrong answer ships |\n79: | A widened type or an added `any` | The type was right; the value is wrong |\n80: | Changing the expected value to match the actual | You just wrote the bug down as the specification |\n81: | A default that papers over a missing value | The producer never set it, and now nobody will find out |\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. WHEN AN ATTEMPT REPEATS\n86: \n87: **If an attempt repeats without producing new evidence, stop patching at the failure\n88: site.** The local retry count is set outside this file. This stop fires on repetition\n89: without new evidence, not on a count.\n90: \n91: Do not repeat the same guess with a variation. Instead:\n92: \n93: 1. **Restate the problem one level up:** at the interface, the data flow, or the module\n94:    boundary rather than the line.\n95: 2. **Inspect the interface that actually exists.** Read the signature, type, schema,\n96:    docs, caller.\n97: 3. **Try once from the new framing.**\n98: \n99: Verify commands, flags, paths and APIs exist before relying on them. When an option turns\n100: out unsupported, read the available interface and change approach, do not re-run the\n101: same command with a different spelling.\n102: \n103: **Naming the seam.** When a fix only works by special-casing a caller, the boundary is\n104: wrong. Say so: **name the seam and the files a seam fix would touch**, then ask.\n105: `scope-discipline.md` still binds, \"the real fix is over there\" does not grant a yes.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. NEVER MAKE A CHECK PASS BY WEAKENING IT\n110: \n111: Not a fix, in any circumstance: skipping, disabling, quarantining or deleting a failing\n112: test; loosening an assertion until it passes; excluding the failing file from the linter,\n113: type checker or matcher; committing an empty change or re-triggering a run for a\n114: different roll.\n115: \n116: If a test is genuinely wrong, that is a finding to raise with evidence, not a line to\n117: edit on your way past.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 5. \"FLAKE\" IS A CONCLUSION, NOT A STARTING HYPOTHESIS\n122: \n123: Only two things support it: the same check passed on this exact input before, or the run\n124: died before any test body executed (checkout, install, runner loss). Absent one of those,\n125: treat the failure as real. One re-run to confirm, at most; a second failure is a bug.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 6. OWNERSHIP\n130: \n131: \"Pre-existing.\" \"Not caused by my changes.\" \"Environment issue.\" These may be true and\n132: are never a stopping point. Diagnose far enough to know which it is, say what you found,\n133: then decide with the operator. A failure you walked past is a failure you shipped.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 7. WHEN YOU ARE STUCK, ESCALATION FORMAT\n138: \n139: Once repetition has replaced evidence and the retry budget is spent, escalate once:\n140: \n141: 1. **Symptom:** the exact error, verbatim.\n142: 2. **What I tried:** each attempt and the evidence it produced, not just what it was.\n143: 3. **What that rules out:** the hypotheses now eliminated.\n144: 4. **Options:** two or three, with the trade-off of each.\n145: 5. **Recommendation:** one, and why.\n146: \n147: A stuck report without evidence per attempt asks someone else to start from zero.\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n152: \n153: - [ ] Reproduced the exact symptom before changing anything.\n154: - [ ] I can state the mechanism in one sentence.\n155: - [ ] The fix is at the producer, not where the symptom surfaced, and I checked that\n156:       producer's other consumers.\n157: - [ ] No test, assertion, or check was weakened to get green.\n158: - [ ] If I called it a flake, I have one of the two pieces of evidence.\n159: - [ ] Re-ran the failing check *and* the whole gate.\n160: - [ ] When an attempt repeated, I restated the problem one level up instead of trying the same guess.\n161: - [ ] A fix that only works by special-casing a caller was reported as a seam, with the files a seam fix would touch, and asked about.\n162: - [ ] I owned the failure I met, and when stuck I escalated in the format with the evidence, the cause if known and the decision needed.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md, lines 1-162\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Root cause and debugging\"\n3: description: \"Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"fix the producer not the symptom\"\n6:   - \"name the mechanism\"\n7:   - \"symptom fix smells\"\n8:   - \"special case\"\n9:   - \"add a retry\"\n10:   - \"add a sleep\"\n11:   - \"broadened catch\"\n12:   - \"loosen the assertion\"\n13:   - \"never weaken a check\"\n14:   - \"make the red green\"\n15:   - \"it's a flake\"\n16:   - \"infra problem\"\n17:   - \"pre-existing failure\"\n18:   - \"not caused by my change\"\n19:   - \"second attempt at the same fix\"\n20:   - \"level up to the seam\"\n21:   - \"stuck escalation format\"\n22:   - \"flaky test\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.2\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Root cause and debugging\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when something is red.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Anything fails: a test, a build, a check, a runtime error, a wrong output.\n36: - You are on the second attempt at the same fix.\n37: - About to add a special case, a retry, a sleep, a broadened `catch`, or a loosened assertion.\n38: - You are tempted to call a failure a flake, an infra problem, or pre-existing.\n39: \n40: ## The rule\n41: \n42: **Fix the producer, not the symptom. Every fix names the mechanism that caused the\n43: failure.**\n44: \n45: If you cannot say *why* it broke in one sentence, you have not found the cause, you\n46: have found a place where the symptom stops appearing.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE LOOP\n51: \n52: 1. **Reproduce the exact symptom** safely. Not a similar one, the same failure, the\n53:    same message.\n54: 2. **Read the whole error.** The top line names where it surfaced; the cause is usually\n55:    the innermost frame in code you control, and the message is usually literal.\n56: 3. **Locate the producer:** the code that created the bad value or state, not the code\n57:    that choked on it.\n58: 4. **Trace to the consumers.** Anything else reading that producer has the same bug,\n59:    surfaced or not.\n60: 5. **Fix at the source.**\n61: 6. **Re-run the same check that failed**, then the whole gate.\n62: \n63: Step 1 is not optional. If you cannot reproduce it you cannot prove you fixed it, and\n64: you should say so instead of claiming a fix (see the negative control in\n65: `evidence-and-proof.md`).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. SYMPTOM-FIX SMELLS\n70: \n71: Each is evidence you are patching in the wrong place:\n72: \n73: | Smell | What it is really telling you |\n74: |-------|-------------------------------|\n75: | A special case for one caller | The seam between the module and that caller is wrong |\n76: | A retry around a deterministic operation | You do not know why it fails, and now it fails five times |\n77: | A `sleep` | A real ordering or lifecycle bug, now timing-dependent |\n78: | A broadened `catch` or bare `except` | The failure is now silent and the wrong answer ships |\n79: | A widened type or an added `any` | The type was right; the value is wrong |\n80: | Changing the expected value to match the actual | You just wrote the bug down as the specification |\n81: | A default that papers over a missing value | The producer never set it, and now nobody will find out |\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. WHEN AN ATTEMPT REPEATS\n86: \n87: **If an attempt repeats without producing new evidence, stop patching at the failure\n88: site.** The local retry count is set outside this file. This stop fires on repetition\n89: without new evidence, not on a count.\n90: \n91: Do not repeat the same guess with a variation. Instead:\n92: \n93: 1. **Restate the problem one level up:** at the interface, the data flow, or the module\n94:    boundary rather than the line.\n95: 2. **Inspect the interface that actually exists.** Read the signature, type, schema,\n96:    docs, caller.\n97: 3. **Try once from the new framing.**\n98: \n99: Verify commands, flags, paths and APIs exist before relying on them. When an option turns\n100: out unsupported, read the available interface and change approach, do not re-run the\n101: same command with a different spelling.\n102: \n103: **Naming the seam.** When a fix only works by special-casing a caller, the boundary is\n104: wrong. Say so: **name the seam and the files a seam fix would touch**, then ask.\n105: `scope-discipline.md` still binds, \"the real fix is over there\" does not grant a yes.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. NEVER MAKE A CHECK PASS BY WEAKENING IT\n110: \n111: Not a fix, in any circumstance: skipping, disabling, quarantining or deleting a failing\n112: test; loosening an assertion until it passes; excluding the failing file from the linter,\n113: type checker or matcher; committing an empty change or re-triggering a run for a\n114: different roll.\n115: \n116: If a test is genuinely wrong, that is a finding to raise with evidence, not a line to\n117: edit on your way past.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 5. \"FLAKE\" IS A CONCLUSION, NOT A STARTING HYPOTHESIS\n122: \n123: Only two things support it: the same check passed on this exact input before, or the run\n124: died before any test body executed (checkout, install, runner loss). Absent one of those,\n125: treat the failure as real. One re-run to confirm, at most; a second failure is a bug.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 6. OWNERSHIP\n130: \n131: \"Pre-existing.\" \"Not caused by my changes.\" \"Environment issue.\" These may be true and\n132: are never a stopping point. Diagnose far enough to know which it is, say what you found,\n133: then decide with the operator. A failure you walked past is a failure you shipped.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 7. WHEN YOU ARE STUCK, ESCALATION FORMAT\n138: \n139: Once repetition has replaced evidence and the retry budget is spent, escalate once:\n140: \n141: 1. **Symptom:** the exact error, verbatim.\n142: 2. **What I tried:** each attempt and the evidence it produced, not just what it was.\n143: 3. **What that rules out:** the hypotheses now eliminated.\n144: 4. **Options:** two or three, with the trade-off of each.\n145: 5. **Recommendation:** one, and why.\n146: \n147: A stuck report without evidence per attempt asks someone else to start from zero.\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n152: \n153: - [ ] Reproduced the exact symptom before changing anything.\n154: - [ ] I can state the mechanism in one sentence.\n155: - [ ] The fix is at the producer, not where the symptom surfaced, and I checked that\n156:       producer's other consumers.\n157: - [ ] No test, assertion, or check was weakened to get green.\n158: - [ ] If I called it a flake, I have one of the two pieces of evidence.\n159: - [ ] Re-ran the failing check *and* the whole gate.\n160: - [ ] When an attempt repeated, I restated the problem one level up instead of trying the same guess.\n161: - [ ] A fix that only works by special-casing a caller was reported as a seam, with the files a seam fix would touch, and asked about.\n162: - [ ] I owned the failure I met, and when stuck I escalated in the format with the evidence, the cause if known and the decision needed."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527775718,"end":1794527776714}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527776718,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","part":{"partID":"prt_1d230ce86001zhf4NRbdYnjkfW","sessionID":"ses_e2dd11607ffeUOdb1Ko5q11ZLP","messageID":"msg_1d230b944001MKvUUH3h0wjMES","type":"tool","id":"call_35f41ee7256e46d49d4d9889","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527776390,"end":1794527776718}}}}
